#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: process-needs-a-detached-spawn
description: "exec waits for the child, so a served program cannot start anything that outlives the request"
---
# `std/process` can only run children it waits for

Reported from rozum. We ported fifteen HTTP routes of a control console from Rust to ScalaScript.
Every route that *starts* something — an agent, a coder session, a terminal, a benchmark run —
stopped at the same wall, and it is not "no process control": it is that the one primitive there is
**waits**.

```text
extern def exec(cmd: String, args: List[String], opts: ProcessOptions): ProcessResult
```

`ProcessResult` carries `stdout`, `stderr` and `exitCode`, so by construction the call cannot return
until the child is finished. A request handler that starts a five-minute agent run has two options
today, and both are wrong: hold the HTTP connection open for five minutes, or never start it.

The program below measures the wall it hits: it execs a child that sleeps two seconds and prints
the elapsed time around the call.

## What would be enough

Not a process supervisor — just a spawn that returns a handle instead of a result:

```text
case class Child(pid: Int)
extern def spawn(cmd: String, args: List[String], opts: ProcessOptions): Child
```

`pid` alone would carry us: the caller records it, and killing is already possible through
`exec("kill", [pid.toString], …)`. Anything more (`wait`, `isAlive`, streams) is welcome but is not
what blocks the port. Detaching from the parent's lifetime matters too — the child must survive the
server that started it, which is what "detached" means here.

## What we do instead

The Rust half of the console keeps every launch route: `std::process::Command::spawn()`, the pid
into a registry file, and the HTTP handler returns immediately. The ScalaScript half serves the
fifteen routes that only read files, call HTTP, or run a child to completion — the boundary is
exactly this primitive.

```scalascript
[exec, ProcessOptions](std/process.ssc)

def main(): Unit =
  println("before exec")
  val r = exec("/bin/sleep", ["2"], ProcessOptions(None, Map(), None, true))
  println("after exec, child exit " + r.exitCode.toString)
  println("the handler could not have answered during those two seconds")
```
